/debug
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int main() {
    int *p;
    *p = 10;
    cout << *p;
}

You correctly declared a pointer. The problem is that `p` is uninitialized, so dereferencing it is unsafe. Give it a valid object to point to first.

```cpp
int x = 0;
int *p = &x;
*p = 10;
```

Now `p` points to `x`. What value does `x` contain afterward?

