#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
void print(const string s) {
    cout << s;
}
int main() {
    print("hello");
}

This probably compiles anyway, because `<iostream>` pulls in `string` on most compilers, but the standard doesn't promise it. Include the header yourself, and pass the string by const reference to avoid a copy.

```cpp
#include <string>
void print(const string &s) { cout << s; }
```

Why is relying on another header to provide `string` risky?

