/debug
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int main() {
    int *p = new int(10);
    delete p;
    cout << *p;
}

You correctly paired `new` with `delete`. The bug is the order: `*p` is read after the object was destroyed. Use the value first, then delete it.

```cpp
int *p = new int(10);
cout << *p;
delete p;
```

Setting `p = nullptr` after `delete` also helps. Why would that make accidental reuse easier to detect?

/debug
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int main() {
    int x = 10;
    int *p = &x;
    delete p;
}

You correctly made `p` point to `x`. The problem is that `delete` is only for memory obtained dynamically with `new`. `x` is an automatic local object.

```cpp
int x = 10;
int *p = &x;
// no delete for p here
```

What should determine whether you use `delete`?

