#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int main() {
    const int x = 10;
    x = 20;
    cout << x;
}

You correctly used `const` to protect `x`. A `const` object can't change after initialization, so the assignment is an error. Either remove it, or don't make `x` const.

```cpp
int x = 10;
x = 20;
```

When is `const` the better choice?

