#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int main() {
    int x = 5;
    const int *p = &x;
    *p = 10;
}

You made the pointer refer to `x`. With `const int *p`, the object can't be changed *through* `p`. Either change `x` directly, or use a non-const pointer.

```cpp
int x = 5;
int *p = &x;
*p = 10;
```

What would change if the declaration were `int *const p` instead?

/debug
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int main() {
    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++);
    {
        cout << i;
    }
}

That part is right: you used the standard three-part `for` loop structure. There are two issues to notice: the semicolon makes the loop body empty, and `i` declared in the loop is scoped to the loop.

```cpp
for (int i = 0; i < 5; ++i) {
    cout << i;
}
```

Why does putting the braces immediately after the `for` change the behavior?

/debug
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int main() {
    int i = 0;
    while (i < 5) {
        cout << i;
    }
}

You wrote the loop condition correctly. The loop never changes `i`, so the condition remains true and the loop does not terminate.

```cpp
while (i < 5) {
    cout << i;
    ++i;
}
```

Now `i` progresses toward 5. Why is changing the loop-control value important?

