#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;
int main() {
    vector<int> v = {1,2,3};
    for (int i = 0; i <= v.size(); ++i)
        cout << v[i];
}

You correctly used `vector` and its `size()` member. The loop condition allows `i` to equal the size, but the last valid index is one less than the size.

```cpp
for (int i = 0; i < v.size(); ++i)
    cout << v[i];
```

Why is `< size()` the natural boundary for zero-based indexing?

