using namespace std;
int main() {
    int x = 10;
    int &r = x;
    int y = 20;
    r = y;
    cout << x;
}

A reference can't be re-bound. `r = y` copies the value of `y` into `x`, so `x` becomes 20. If you need something that can be re-pointed, use a pointer.

```cpp
int *p = &x;
p = &y;
```

Which one can be `nullptr`: a reference or a pointer?

