#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int main() {
    int x = 10;
    if (x > 5)
        cout << "big";
        cout << "done";
}

You wrote the condition correctly. Without braces, only the next statement belongs to the `if`; indentation does not change that.

```cpp
if (x > 5) {
    cout << "big";
    cout << "done";
}
```

Braces make both statements part of the conditional block. When are braces especially helpful?

/debug
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int main() {
    bool ready = true;
    if (ready = false)
        cout << "ready";
}

Nice: you correctly used a boolean variable. The condition assigns `false` to `ready` instead of comparing the two values.

```cpp
if (ready == false)
    cout << "ready";
```

You could also write `if (!ready)`. What does the `!` operator do to a boolean?

